 "raw_reply": "{\n  \"experiment_id\": \"EXP-029\",\n  \"observed_evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"extra_trees has the highest mean ROC-AUC of the five baseline models in the interactions stage: 0.7769, fold std 0.0298, min 0.7430, max 0.7993.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-029 / evidence.model_results[model=extra_trees].metrics.roc_auc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Mean ROC-AUC for the other models: hist_gradient_boosting 0.7673 (std 0.0321), xgboost 0.7624 (std 0.0426), lightgbm 0.7601 (std 0.0328), logistic_regression 0.7479 (std 0.0481).\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-029 / evidence.model_results[*].metrics.roc_auc.mean and .std\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The selection rule is mean ROC-AUC minus 0.25×fold std, then runtime, on training CV only.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-029 / evidence.selection_rule\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Every model was evaluated with 3 folds, 1 repeat, empty model_params, and optimization 'baseline', so no model was tuned.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-029 / evidence.model_results[*].folds, .repeats, .model_params, .optimization\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"All results come from a single feature stage ('interactions') with a mean of 56 features. No other stage, such as raw features, appears in this bundle.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-029 / evidence.model_results[*].stage, evidence.model_results[*].feature_count_mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Per-fold ROC-AUC: extra_trees 0.7993 / 0.7883 / 0.7430; hist_gradient_boosting 0.8037 / 0.7554 / 0.7428; xgboost 0.8116 / 0.7390 / 0.7368. In fold 1 hist_gradient_boosting and xgboost both exceed extra_trees. In fold 3 extra_trees leads hist_gradient_boosting by about 0.0002.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-029 / evidence.model_results[*].fold_metrics[*].roc_auc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"extra_trees has the lowest mean log loss (0.5084), the lowest mean Brier score (0.1684), and the lowest mean ECE-10 (0.0526) of the five models.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-029 / evidence.model_results[model=extra_trees].metrics.log_loss.mean, .brier.mean, .ece_10.mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"At the fixed 0.5 threshold, extra_trees has mean recall 0.375 and mean F1 0.468. hist_gradient_boosting has recall 0.454 and F1 0.519.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-029 / evidence.model_results[model=extra_trees|hist_gradient_boosting].metrics.recall.mean, .f1.mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Feature stability varies by model. Many interaction features appear in only 1 of 3 folds. For extra_trees, inter_Attribute1_x_ratio_Attribute2_div_Attribute13 was observed in 1 fold. The hist_gradient_boosting feature_stability list is empty.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-029 / evidence.model_results[*].feature_stability\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The bundle's limitations list is empty. The claim's own limitation says the leader has not seen the locked holdout.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-029 / limitations; EXP-029-C1 / claims[0].limitations\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"interpretation\": \"Inference, derived from the cited numbers: applying the stated rule gives approximately extra_trees 0.769, hist_gradient_boosting 0.759, lightgbm 0.752, xgboost 0.752, logistic_regression 0.736. Extra_trees therefore ranks first under the rule. The mean-AUC gap to hist_gradient_boosting is about 0.0096, roughly a third of a single model's fold std, and it is based on only 3 folds with 1 repeat. The fold-level ordering is not consistent (hist_gradient_boosting and xgboost lead in fold 1; extra_trees and hist_gradient_boosting are essentially tied in fold 3). The evidence supports extra_trees as a provisional training-CV leader among untuned baselines. It does not show that tree ensembles or extra_trees are meaningfully better than hist_gradient_boosting. Extra_trees' better calibration and log loss are a secondary observation, but the selection rule does not use them. No claim about feature effects or causes is supported, since importances are predictive and fold-unstable.\",\n  \"decision\": \"Retain extra_trees as a provisional training-CV candidate only. Treat extra_trees and hist_gradient_boosting as statistically unresolved. Do not describe extra_trees as an algorithm-family winner, do not promote it to the locked holdout until the paired repeated-CV check below passes, and do not approve any feature or deployment.\",\n  \"risks_and_missing_evidence\": [\n    \"Only 3 folds and 1 repeat (EXP-029 / evidence.model_results[*].folds, .repeats). The fold std and ci95 are very noisy at n=3, and the gap between extra_trees and the runner-up is small relative to that noise.\",\n    \"No paired fold-wise comparison or corrected resampled test is reported. Whether the models share identical splits is not stated in the bundle.\",\n    \"All models use default parameters (model_params {}, optimization 'baseline'). Tuning sensitivity differs by family, so a ranking of untuned defaults is not a ranking of algorithm families.\",\n    \"The claim says 'using interactions features', but the bundle has no raw-feature or other-stage comparison. It cannot show that interactions help, and the stage may have been chosen on the same CV folds, creating selection optimism.\",\n    \"The bundle does not show that feature construction (ratios, log1p, interactions) and any feature selection were fit inside each training fold. If the ratio or interaction feature set was chosen using all training rows, the CV estimates could be optimistic. The 1-of-3-fold appearance of interaction features suggests some per-fold selection, but this is not confirmed.\",\n    \"No decision-time or production-availability review exists for any feature, including the engineered ratios and interactions. No feature is approved.\",\n    \"The Attribute1..Attribute20 names are anonymized. In the common Statlog German Credit encoding, some of these (for example age and personal status/sex) could be sensitive or proxy attributes. Several appear in the top-importance lists, for example Attribute13 ratios in lightgbm and Attribute9 in extra_trees and logistic_regression. If that mapping holds, a fairness and permissibility review is needed.\",\n    \"The selection metric is ROC-AUC only. Thresholded metrics at 0.5 show low recall for extra_trees (mean 0.375), and no cost-sensitive or operating-point analysis is provided. The decision-relevant cost asymmetry for credit risk is unaddressed.\",\n    \"Calibration advantages for extra_trees (ECE, log loss, Brier) come from 3 folds and are not part of the selection rule. They are not tested for significance.\",\n    \"Feature importance is not comparable across model families, and rankings are fold-unstable. They must not be read as causal or as stable drivers.\",\n    \"The holdout has not been used (per the claim limitation), so generalization is untested. Dataset size and the stratification or grouping used are not given in the bundle, so duplicate or group leakage cannot be ruled out.\"\n  ],\n  \"challenges_to_claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-029-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The claim names extra_trees as the candidate with ROC-AUC 0.7769±0.0298. The ranking margin over hist_gradient_boosting (0.7673±0.0321) is far smaller than the fold std, so 'strongest stable' is not supported at 3 folds × 1 repeat. Fold-level ranks also disagree (EXP-029 / evidence.model_results[*].fold_metrics[*].roc_auc).\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-029-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"'Using interactions features' implies the interactions stage is part of the selected configuration, but no other feature stage is shown for comparison, so the benefit of the interactions stage is unsupported. The leakage-safety of the engineered features (fold-internal fitting) and their decision-time availability are also unreviewed.\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-029-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"Models are untuned baselines (optimization 'baseline', empty model_params). A single-split-protocol ranking of defaults does not justify a family-level conclusion for the dataset profile. The claim's own limitation concedes it is not a universal winner.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-029-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The selection rule uses ROC-AUC alone. Extra_trees has lower recall and F1 at the 0.5 threshold than hist_gradient_boosting, lightgbm, and xgboost (EXP-029 / evidence.model_results[*].metrics.recall.mean, .f1.mean). It is therefore not shown to be best for any specific operating point or cost structure. Its better calibration is observed but not part of the rule.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-029-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The evidence contains no per-feature decision-time or availability review and potentially sensitive attributes appear among top features. Feature-importance lists must not be used to justify features or imply drivers.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"next_experiment\": {\n    \"title\": \"Paired repeated-CV re-test of extra_trees vs hist_gradient_boosting with a raw-feature control\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"If extra_trees on interaction features is a genuinely stronger training-CV candidate, it will beat hist_gradient_boosting in paired ROC-AUC across repeated, identically split CV, and will not do worse than its raw-feature variant. If the 3-fold gap was noise, the paired difference will be indistinguishable from zero.\",\n    \"success_gate\": \"Using repeated stratified 5-fold × 5 repeats with identical splits for all arms, with all feature construction and selection inside each training fold: (a) the paired mean ROC-AUC difference (extra_trees interactions minus hist_gradient_boosting interactions) is > 0 with a corrected resampled 95% CI (Nadeau-Bengio) or paired bootstrap lower bound > 0; AND (b) extra_trees on interaction features is not lower than extra_trees on raw features by more than 0.005 mean ROC-AUC. If (a) fails, declare extra_trees and hist_gradient_boosting statistically tied and carry both forward. If (b) fails, drop the interactions claim for extra_trees.\",\n    \"why_smallest\": \"It reuses the same dataset, metric, selection rule and the two closest competitors, and adds only one control arm (raw features). It tests the two weakest points in the claim, the noise-level margin and the unsupported interactions attribution, without tuning, new features, or touching the locked holdout.\"\n  },\n  \"overall_confidence\": \"low\"\n}",
